    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Weight pressure from coaches and teammates was a significant predictor of both bulimic tendencies and the drive for muscularity. Appearance pressures predicted athletes drive for muscularity, it did not predict bulimic symptomatology."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "A necessary next step in the advancement of the WPS-M is to further assess the two factor structure of the instrument by administering it to new sample of male athletes, and conducting confirmatory factor analysis on their responses. Samples in excess of 20 cases per item may further strenghten the generalizibility and replicability of the results."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Longitudinal studies will allow for an investigation of the predictive validity and test-retest reliability of the measure to continue the psychometric testing of the WPS-M. Future studies can allow for the inclusion of athletes from additional sports. Particularly for males is aesthetic sports, appearance pressures may prove to be an even more salient predictor of disordered eating, as appearance and performance are closely connected in sports such as diving and gymnastics. \nMale athletes often lack the vocabulary to discuss body image concerns, therefore clinicians need to be savy in helping them uncover their personal connections between body expectations, stress and performance."
    },
